today we'll learn how to do three  distinct things in flutter first we'll  capture an image from the device using  the camera or photo gallery next we'll  allow the user to crop rotate or scale  the image file and lastly we'll upload  the file to a cloud storage bucket and  we'll even throw in a progress bar and a  pause resume button just because it's so  easy to do with flutter and firebase if  you're new here like and subscribe and  follow along with the full write-up on  fire ship I own let's start by taking a  closer look at what we'll build over the  next few minutes you can see down here  in the bottom bar we have two icon  buttons tapping the camera will bring up  the device camera and the image will  bring up the image gallery when the user  selects an image it will give us access  to that file object in our dart code  when the user taps the crop button it  will bring up the image crop or widget  allowing the user to manipulate the  image and that magic is made possible by  the awesome image crop rap legend and  lastly when the user taps the upload to  firebase button it will immediately  start uploading the file to a firebase  storage bucket the progress indicator  will show the percentage of bytes  transferred while the file is uploading  and the user can also control the state  of the upload by pausing or resuming  them so that's a lot of functionality  for a single YouTube video but I think  you might be pleasantly surprised at how  easy this stuff is to implement to get  started you'll need to have a flutter  app with firebase installed I'm not  going to cover the firebase setup  instructions in this video but you can  follow the official instructions or take  the full flutter course as a fire ship  Pro member now if we go ahead and jump  into the pub spec yamo file you can see  we have firebase core and storage these  are the only two firebase dependencies  that you need if your only concern is  uploading files to a cloud storage  bucket but in our case we also want the  user to interact with the native device  camera and image gallery which we can do  with image picker and then image cropper  will allow the user to then manipulate  the image after it's been selected and  these plugins do have a small amount of  platform specific configuration when it  comes to image picker you'll need to go  into your info.plist file for iOS and  add the corresponding permissions for  the photo library and camera and for  image cropper there's no config for iOS  but you do need to add an activity to  your Android manifest file and you  should always refer to the official  documentation when installing a new  plugin in flutter now moving over to our  main dart file we'll go ahead and import  these dependencies and the only other  unusual thing is that we're importing  dart IO and that gives us access to the  file class which we'll see in use here  in a couple minutes now the app or  building has two very different concerns  with one being the image capture from  the device and the other being the file  upload to a remote storage  and we can easily separate these  concerns and flutterby creating two  different stateful widgets the first one  that we'll build is called image capture  and it's responsibility is to capture an  image from the gallery or camera and  then allow the user to crop and resize  it so the only piece of state on the  switch --it is the actual file itself  and the image file state will change  when the user selects an image or when  they crop an image the first method will  define as pic image which will either  allow the user to select an image from  the image gallery or capture a new image  with the camera the image picker plug-in  makes this dead simple all we do is  write a single line of code and pass at  the source which will tell it whether or  not to use the camera or the gallery and  the result of this future will be the  image file which we can set as the state  on this widget and that's all it takes  to capture an image from the native  device now the other way the image files  State might change is if the user crops  an image using the image crop or plugin  and using this plug-in is just as easy  we simply pass in the source path of the  existing image and it will take that  existing image and create a newly  cropped or modified image and you also  have a bunch of optional properties that  you can pass to the plug-in as well to  customize its behavior if the user  confirms the modification it will result  in a new image file which we can then  set as the image file state but if the  user cancels the modifications the value  will be null in which case we can use  darts nola where double question mark  operator to return the existing image  file now at this point you might be  wondering how we're getting so much  amazing functionality with only a few  lines of code  the magic is made possible by the image  copper plug-in which itself is a wrapper  for native libraries like you crop and  tío crop view controller on iOS now that  we have the logic in place to capture  and crop an image let's move on to the  UI we have a couple of buttons down here  at the bottom which allow the user to  either capture from the camera or from  the gallery and then once an image is  selected they can either crop it or  clear it by simply setting the image  file to null we can implement the  buttons down at the bottom by setting up  a scaffold and then defining a bottom  navigation bar as a bottom app bar and  we'll create a row with a couple of icon  buttons in it and each icon button will  call the same pick image method just  with a different argument for the source  which can either be the camera or the  gallery from there we'll move on to the  body of the scaffold which we'll define  as a ListView and then it will have  children if the image file does not  equal null  if we have an image file then we'll go  ahead and show it in the UI with the  image widget and then we'll display a  row with some buttons that allow the  user to crop that image or to clear it  from the UI and that code can be  implemented by simply attaching the  methods that we defined earlier as the  unpressed event handlers for these  buttons now the last widget that we'll  add here is called uploader which is a  custom widget that will define next and  we'll pass the image file down to it as  an input property the responsibility of  the uploader is to take the raw file and  then create a firebase storage upload  task one of the benefits of having this  in its own widget is that you could  potentially a loop over it to create  multiple upload tasks at once which  would allow you to upload multiple files  concurrently we can pass the file object  from the parent to the child by setting  it as a property here and then adding it  to the constructor from there we'll go  down to the state and will instantiate  firebase storage you can think of this  as a service that allows you to interact  with a cloud storage bucket to both  upload and download files you just need  to pass at a storage bucket argument  with the bucket URI which you can find  on the firebase console and what we're  here in the console I should also point  out that you should have security rules  disabled for the storage bucket just so  we can freely upload files for this demo  now back in our code we'll set a  property for the storage upload task  this is the single most important part  of the feature because it provides the  current state of the upload as well as  the event stream that we can listen to  to update the UI when you instantiate a  storage upload task it will immediately  start uploading that file to storage so  we'll go ahead and create a start upload  method that allows the user to click a  button to start the upload lazily in  addition to the file you also need to  have a path to upload that file to a  storage bucket is no different than the  file system on your computer and that  means only one file can exist at a given  path at a time many apps will generate a  unique ID for each individual upload or  a quick and dirty way to do it is just  to use date/time now so now that we have  the file path string and the raw file  object we can make a reference to the  storage bucket then call child with the  file path and then call put file with  the file object and that will return us  with a firebase storage upload task and  that's actually all it takes to upload a  file to storage but you'll very likely  want to give the user a progress  indicator and maybe the option to pause  or cancel the upload as well in the  build method we'll go ahead and start  with a conditional and if the  the upload task is null then we'll  display a button that allows the user to  start the upload so tapping this button  will create an instance of an upload  task which will immediately start  transmitting that file to your firebase  storage bucket the upload task object  also exposes a stream of storage tasks  events which we can pass to a stream  builder to update the progress bar so  we'll use a storage task event as the  generic type to the stream builder and  we'll set the stream property as the  upload task events the storage task  event object contains useful information  like the number of bytes transferred and  the total bytes in the file so you can  calculate the total progress of the  upload and you can expect the stream to  emit a new event every couple hundred  milliseconds or so now when it comes to  a progress bar we'll be looking for a  number that ranges from zero to one and  we can obtain the information to make  that calculation from the event  it contains the number of bytes  transferred to storage thus far along  with a bunch of other metadata that you  might find useful in our case here we  can get the progress percent by dividing  the total by count by the bytes  transferred now one of the really cool  things about firebase is that it makes  it easy for the user to pause cancel and  resume and upload and we can control  which parts of the UI to show based on  the state of the upload task that means  you can set up some conditional logic  and then use the different states like  is canceled as complete is in progress  etc in addition to the state the upload  tasks also contains methods for the user  to manually control the upload for  example if the upload task is paused  then we'll go ahead and create a button  that allows them to resume it and then  we can do the exact opposite if the  upload task is in progress  then we'll provide a button to pause it  and last but not least we'll want to  show the user some kind of progress  indicator an easy way to do this is with  the built in linear progress indicator  and we can use the progress percent that  we figured out earlier as its value and  for an added touch we'll go ahead and  display the progress percent as a string  as well and that's all it takes to build  a full stack file upload feature with  flutter I'm gonna go ahead and wrap  things up there if this video helped you  please like and subscribe and if you  want to learn more about flutter and  firebase consider enrolling in the full  flutter course or becoming a pro member  at fire ship i/o thanks for watching and  I will talk to you soon  [Music]

